WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Robi Taxis just launched in London with ai behind the wheel. Here's what that actually means for everyday travel. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 155. Uber just started running its first robot Taxis in London, and they are already rolling through real city streets. Robot Taxis are cars that can drive themselves using artificial intelligence or ai. ai here means computer software that learns patterns and then chooses actions. Instead of following one

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.240
fixed script, a human supervisor is still in the vehicle for now, which is an important safety detail. Think of it like a very careful autopilot that watches the road through cameras and sensors. It predicts what other cars and pedestrians will do, then steers, slows, or waits. This autopilot is running on powerful computers instead of a human brain. The system processes constant streams of visual data to build an up-to-date picture of everything nearby.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:24.940
Then it decides on the next safe move, like slowing for a pedestrian or changing lanes around a cyclist. Picture a driver who can look left, right, forward, and behind at the exact same moment. That is the promise of these sensors, acting like extra sets of eyes that don't get tired. Crossing a busy road means tracking bikes, buses, and people all at once, and these cars attempt that job every second.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:52.260
The big shift is that ai is leaving apps and games and entering real city streets. For students, this could eventually change trips to school, sports practice, or meeting friends. Rides could become cheaper and more available at any hour, not just when a driver is free. It also raises hard questions about safety and jobs that don't have simple answers yet. If ai can drive, what happens to taxi and delivery drivers over time?

00:01:52.260 --> 00:02:16.060
Cities testing these vehicles are learning how the technology handles unexpected situations. That includes construction zones or sudden weather changes. The messy stuff, no demo video, fully captures. The human supervisor acts as an extra layer of safety while the ai learns from real-world miles. Think of that person as a driving instructor in the passenger seat, ready to grab the wheel.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:45.440
This matters because transportation is something everyone uses, whether they ride, walk, or bike beside it. The rules being tested in London today will likely spread to other cities later. These vehicles may show up on roads sooner than most people think. Understanding how they work helps with forming real opinions about the future, instead of only reacting to headlines. Right now, the service is limited and supervised, so these are still early days, not a full takeover of taxis.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:03:10.520
Watching the rollout gives a front row seat to ai in action, without needing any special app yet. If you want something concrete to do, open Uber's own site or local London transport news and look for robot taxi updates. Notice where the cars are allowed to run and whether a human is still required inside. That simple check is how to watch ai move from a cool idea to an actual street.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:36.720
The part that still feels like a magic trick is how a car can understand it a street at all. Pull up a racing game in your mind, the kind played on a phone or a console. The computer has to keep track of your car, the other cars, the track edges, and any obstacles all at once. It doesn't only react to what it sees right this second. It also guesses what might happen next, like whether that car ahead is about to slow down or swerve.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:59.940
Now put that same habit on real roads instead of a bright game screen. The ai in a self-driving car uses cameras, radar, and other sensors as its eyes. Radar bounces signals off objects to judge distance, a bit like hearing an echo, and knowing how far the wall is. Those eyes turn everything they see into a constantly updating map of the world around the car.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:24.220
Then the system runs predictions. Little, what if questions? Many times per second. If that pedestrian steps off the curb, how fast will they move? If the light turns yellow, should the car break or keep going? The system doesn't make one giant decision at a time, which would be too slow and too brittle. It breaks the problem into smaller pieces, like stations in a busy kitchen during a dinner rush.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:45.240
One part spots objects, one part forecasts their movement, one part chooses a safe path, and another adjusts steering and speed. They all share notes constantly, so the trip doesn't fall apart. When something unexpected appears, like a ball rolling into the street, the ai has to weigh options fast and pick the safest one.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:58.100
A game can hide those surprises until a later level, but a real street does not wait. The real world is messy, so the ai faces millions of tiny variations it never saw in exactly the same way.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:13.260
Training is the practice period, when the system studies huge numbers of driving examples so later patterns feel familiar. Over time, the ai improves by learning from every mile driven. Each small correction becomes a better future choice,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:29.000
like a player who actually learns from crashing into the same wall. The car is playing that prediction game, only the track is a living city with people who don't follow a script. Once you see the loop of seeing, guessing and choosing, the mystery gets a lot smaller.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:41.460
It is still serious engineering, but the core idea is one already familiar from games. Meanwhile, ai is also sneaking into the most old school homework imaginable, which is ancient stories.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:56.540
A creative tool called Fable 5.1 was used to build a fully explorable 3D catalog of ships from the Iliad. The Iliad is a classic school text, packed with mythology, long lists, and history that can feel stuck on a page.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:07.360
This project turns that catalog into something a person can actually walk around and investigate. There is a map, accurate ship models, and archaeological images to click through.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:19.660
Archaeological just means the pictures come from real historical digs and museums, not from guesswork. It is like being handed a museum gallery of ancient ships, except the gallery fits on a phone.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:28.600
People can zoom in on models, read background facts, and see how the ai helped keep visuals and information consistent across the whole experience.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:44.260
Here is a challenge that is free and needs zero coding. On a phone or laptop, search for the 3D catalog of ships from the Iliad. Start by picking a ship from the list, then rotate the 3D model slowly, like turning an object in your hands.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:53.020
After that, open the linked images and compare them with the model. Notice whether the details stay consistent from the map to the ship to the photographs.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:07:10.360
That consistency is a big part of what the ai was used to help with. If history, mythology, or cool 3D projects are your thing, treat it like a scavenger hunt. Find one fact about a vessel that is new to you, and one detail on the model that matches an archaeological image.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:25.380
This kind of project shows how ai can help bring old stories to life without needing any coding skills. New York City, the largest school system in the United States, is pausing generative ai tools for students through 8th grade for the coming year.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:33.640
Generative ai means software that creates new text, images, or other content, and the pause covers all public schools in the city.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:40.000
It is like hitting pause on a new game so teachers and families can test guidelines before anyone hits start again.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:54.720
The Department of Justice filed a letter backing open ai in a copyright case with the New York Times. It argues that training ai on copyrighted books and articles can count as fair use, meaning some uses of existing work should be allowed.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:08.020
The outcome may influence how future ai systems are built, like a student learning from library books and then writing something new. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool, go play with it.

